  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Christian Masculinity shows early but meaningful scholarly and public reach: it has been cited four times, downloaded 3,036 times through OAPEN, and appears in teaching-related attention via a recorded YouTube discussion. Its subject also aligns clearly with SDG 5 on gender equality, reflecting the book\u2019s relevance to current debates on religion, gender, and identity.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Yvonne Maria Werner\u2019s <em>Christian Masculinity</em> examines how Christian faith and masculinity have been understood in modern European contexts, addressing a question that sits at the intersection of religious studies, gender studies, and sociology. The impact record suggests that the book has begun to travel across these overlapping conversations rather than remaining within a single disciplinary lane. Its four OpenAlex citations, while still a modest base, indicate that it is already being taken up in related scholarship on religion, gender, and society.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s strongest reach signal is usage: 3,036 downloads recorded through OAPEN point to sustained reading interest. That kind of access is especially notable for a scholarly book whose themes invite both academic and broader interpretive attention. The record also shows two Crossref mentions in public discourse sources, which suggests some visibility beyond citation networks, although the available data do not support a broader media claim.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a clear teaching-adoption signal. The book is discussed in a YouTube video, indicating that it has entered wider educational or interpretive circulation. In addition, its alignment with gender equality underscores its policy and societal relevance, especially given the book\u2019s focus on the historical construction of religion and masculinity and the way these categories shape public life.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record portrays <em>Christian Masculinity</em> as a book with early scholarly uptake, substantial open-access usage, and emerging presence in public-facing discussion. The evidence is concentrated in a small number of signals rather than a large volume of citations, so the most accurate reading is that the book is gaining traction in an active and contemporary research area while its longer-term influence is still developing.</p>",
